      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Method"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "orderedList",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Heat the olive oil in a large stockpot over medium heat. Add the onion and celery and cook for 8-10 minutes, stirring, until softened."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Add the garlic, ground coriander, cumin, turmeric, cinnamon and black pepper. Stir for 1 minute until fragrant."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Add the tinned tomatoes and stir well, breaking them up with a spoon. Cook for 5 minutes."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Add the red lentils and 1.5 litres of water. Bring to the boil, then reduce to a gentle simmer and cook for 20 minutes until the lentils are soft."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Add the drained chickpeas and cook for a further 10 minutes to heat through and allow the flavours to meld."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Stir in the lemon juice and most of the fresh parsley and coriander. Taste and adjust the seasoning. Serve in deep bowls with the remaining herbs scattered on top."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "troubleshooter",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "troubleshooterItem",
            "attrs": {
              "question": "The soup is too thick"
            },
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Add a splash of boiling water and stir well. Harira thickens as it sits, so you will likely need to add a little water when reheating leftovers the following day."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "troubleshooterItem",
            "attrs": {
              "question": "The lentils have not dissolved"
            },
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Red lentils should dissolve after 20-25 minutes of simmering. If yours are still holding shape, simply continue cooking and stir occasionally to help them break down into the broth."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "troubleshooterItem",
            "attrs": {
              "question": "The soup tastes flat"
            },
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "A flat harira almost always needs more lemon juice and salt. Add both gradually, tasting as you go. Fresh herbs stirred in just before serving also brighten the flavour significantly."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Where this dish lives"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "marks": [
              {
                "type": "glossaryTooltip",
                "attrs": {
                  "termSlug": "harira-term"
                }
              }
            ],
            "text": "Harira"
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": " is eaten year-round but is most closely associated with Ramadan, when a bowl of it traditionally breaks the daily fast at sunset. Every household has its own version: some include lamb, some add rice or vermicelli, some thicken with a flour and water slurry called tedouira. This vegetarian version holds close to the classic base of tomatoes, lentils and chickpeas. It is a deeply practical soup, made from ingredients that keep well in the cupboard and that feed a crowd economically."
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
  }
